\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Corollary}
\title{Polynomial Euclidean field insertions}
\date{2026-11-09}

\p{The vacuum and thermal formulas of \ref{fdip-00HB} remain
well-defined for polynomial multiplication observables at strictly
ordered times. Equal-time position products can be combined into
one polynomial. Their one-time expectations have the moment and
trace interpretation of \ref{fdip-00H6}.}

\proof{
\p{The pointwise comparison
#{K_t(x,y)\le e^{bt}K_t^{\mathrm{osc}}(x,y)} of
\ref{fdip-00H6} bounds a thermal integrand in absolute value by
a polynomial times a product of oscillator Mehler kernels.
With positive time intervals, that product is a nondegenerate Gaussian
in all inserted coordinates: each quadratic edge form has positive
eigenvalues proportional to #{\cosh(\omega t)-1} and
#{\cosh(\omega t)+1}. On a vacuum interval, use also the Gaussian
bounds for #{\psi_0} at both endpoints. Thus every absolute
polynomial integral is finite. Bounded value truncations converge
by dominated convergence and give the displayed Euclidean laws.}

\p{The operator interpretation retains every positive heat interval.
By \ref{fdip-00H6}, #{M_pe^{-tH}} is Hilbert–Schmidt for any
polynomial #{p} and #{t>0}. On a thermal cycle with at least two
insertions, the product of two such factors is trace class and
all remaining factors are bounded. For one insertion, split the
heat interval in half and use the positive sandwich or its complex
linear extension from \ref{fdip-00H6}. On a vacuum interval,
the right endpoint vector #{M_p\psi_0} is in #{\mathcal H},
and each preceding positive heat interval supplies the required
bounded multiplication-heat factor. Bounded value truncations converge in these Hilbert–Schmidt norms
by the same diagonal majorant; the endpoint vectors converge in
#{L^2}. The products therefore converge in trace norm on a cycle
and in norm on a vacuum interval, giving the stated operator
meanings for the integral limits.}
}

\p{This conclusion concerns Euclidean position fields. A product
of unbounded fields at real times still requires its actual
successive operator domains; finite Euclidean moments alone do
not define it.}
